als","k":"bank","la":34.691138,"lo":135.194724,"d":1002},{"n":"Toyo Securities","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":34.6904,"lo":135.196262,"d":1002},{"n":"日新信用金庫 宇治川支店","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":34.684329,"lo":135.178647,"d":1048},{"n":"Sundrug","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":34.692003,"lo":135.193668,"d":1055},{"n":"スギドラッグ","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":34.691885,"lo":135.194082,"d":1056},{"n":"Meriken Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.681667,"lo":135.188333,"d":212,"ds":"Meriken Park (メリケンパーク, Meriken pāku) is a waterfront park located in the port city of Kobe, Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan. The park features the Kobe Port Tower, Kobe Maritime Museum, and a memorial to victims of the Great Hanshin earthquake. The name of the park comes from the word \"American,\" which was commonly translated as \"Meriken\" during the Meiji era.","wpu":"Meriken Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2c/Kobe_Mosaic03s4s4272.jpg/960px-Kobe_Mosaic03s4s4272.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kobe Anpanman Children's Museum & Mall","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.678611,"lo":135.185,"d":668,"ds":"The Kobe Anpanman Children's Museum & Mall (神戸アンパンマンこどもミュージアム＆モール) is an interactive children's museum located in Kobe, Japan dedicated to Takashi Yanase's popular Anpanman franchise. It is one of five such complexes in the country (the others being in Yokohama, Sendai, Nagoya and Fukuoka), and also the newest, having been opened in 2013.","wpu":"Kobe Anpanman Children's Museum & Mall","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/130720_Kobe_Anpanman_Children%27s_Museum_%26_Mall_Kobe_Japan01s3.jpg/960px-130720_Kobe_Anpanman_Children%27s_Museum_%26_Mall_Kobe_Japan01s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kobe Bridge","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.67899,"lo":135.20319,"d":1285,"ds":"Kobe Bridge (Japanese: 神戸大橋, Kōbe Ōhashi), also known as Kobe Ohashi Bridge, is a double-deck steel arch bridge in Kobe, Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan. Construction was completed in March 1970, and the bridge spans Kobe Harbour, connecting Shinkō Pier No. 4 on the mainland to the artificial island of Port Island.","wpu":"Kobe Bridge","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Kobe_Bridge_west.jpg/960px-Kobe_Bridge_west.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ikuta Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.695,"lo":135.190556,"d":1335,"ds":"Ikuta Shrine (生田神社, Ikuta-jinja) is a Shinto shrine in the Chūō Ward of Kobe, Japan, and is possibly among the oldest shrines in the country. It was founded by Empress Jingu when she returned from the Three Kan (三韓, Korea) campaign. She was nearly shipwrecked but managed to survive thanks to praying to Watatsumi, and she made the shrine to honor him.","wpu":"Ikuta Shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/54/Ikuta_Shrine_honden.jpg/960px-Ikuta_Shrine_honden.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Minatogawa Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.681,"lo":135.175,"d":1389,"ds":"Minatogawa Shrine (湊川神社, Minatogawa-jinja) is a Shinto shrine in Chūō-ku, Kobe, Japan, roughly on the site of the Battle of Minatogawa established in 1872. The enshrined kami is the spirit of Masashige Kusunoki, a military commander. It is one of the Fifteen Shrines of the Kenmu Restoration.","wpu":"Minatogawa Shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Minatogawa-jinja_shinmon.jpg/960px-Minatogawa-jinja_shinmon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kobe Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.696111,"lo":135.187778,"d":1471,"ds":"The Kōbe Mosque (Japanese: 神戸モスク, romanized: Kōbe Mosuku), also known as the Kōbe Muslim Mosque (Japanese: 神戸ムスリムモスク, romanized: Kōbe Musurimu Mosuku, is a mosque located in Kōbe, in the Hyōgo Prefecture of Japan. Founded in October 1935, it is Japan's first mosque. Its construction was funded by donations collected by the Islamic Committee of Kōbe from 1928.","wpu":"Kobe Mosque","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Kobe_mosque01_2816.jpg/960px-Kobe_mosque01_2816.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nōfuku-ji","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.66822,"lo":135.171392,"d":2365,"ds":"Nōfuku-ji (能福寺) is a Buddhist temple that, from the legend, was founded in 805 by the monk Saichō, in Kita Sakasegawa, Hyōgo-ku, Kobe, Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan. Saichō (of the Tendai sect) placed a statue of Yakushi Nyorai of his own making in the temple hall and named the temple Nōfuku Gokoku Mitsu-ji (能福護国密寺).","wpu":"Nōfuku-ji","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Tsukinowa-eiden.JPG/960px-Tsukinowa-eiden.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Takenaka Carpentry Tools Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.7068,"lo":135.1975,"d":2733,"ds":"The Takenaka Carpentry Tools Museum is a museum of carpentry tools in Kobe, Japan. The museum was opened in 1984 with the objective of collecting and conserving ancient tools as an example of Japanese cultural heritage, in order to pass them on to the next generation through research and exhibitions.","wpu":"Takenaka Carpentry Tools Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/160312_Takenaka_Carpentry_Tools_Museum_Kobe_Japan03s3.jpg/960px-160312_Takenaka_Carpentry_Tools_Museum_Kobe_Japan03s3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Port Island","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.666667,"lo":135.216667,"d":3040,"ds":"Port Island (ポートアイランド, Pōto Airando) is an artificial island in Chuo-ku, Kobe, Japan. It was constructed between 1966 and 1980 (Phase 1) and between 1987 and 2009 (Phase 2) at the Port of Kobe, and officially opened with an exposition called \"Portopia '81.\" It now houses a heliport, numerous hotels, a large convention center, the UCC Coffee Museum, Japan's third IKEA store, and several parks.","wpu":"Port Island","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/45/PortIslandCloseup_2003262.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Hyōgo Prefectural Museum of Art","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":34.699192,"lo":135.218011,"d":3130,"ds":"The Hyōgo Prefectural Museum of Art (兵庫県立美術館, Hyōgo Kenritsu Bijutsukan) is a purpose built municipal art gallery in Nada-ku, Kobe, Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan. It was opened in 2002. The major collections of the museum are foreign and Japanese sculptures, foreign and Japanese prints, Western-style and Japanese-style paintings associated with Hyogo Prefecture, Japanese greatworks in modern era, and contemporary art.","wpu":"Hyōgo Prefectural Museum of Art","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Hyogo_prefectural_museum_of_art08s3200.jpg/960px-Hyogo_prefectural_museum_of_art08s3200.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Kobe , officially Kobe City (神戸市, Kōbe-shi; [koː.beꜜ.ɕi]), is the capital city of Hyōgo Prefecture, Japan. With a population of around 1.5 million, Kobe is Japan's seventh-largest city and the third-largest port city after Tokyo and Yokohama. It is located in the Kansai region, which makes up the southern side of the main island of Honshū, on the north shore of Osaka Bay.","wpu":"Kobe","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/20190901_Kobe_harbor-1.jpg/960px-20190901_Kobe_harbor-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}